{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl9\pnlcrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}\pard\plain \s18\sb100\sa100\keepn\widctlpar \b\f4\fs48\lang1031\kerning36 Advanced PHP: Web Applications - Sessions and Authentication 
\par \pard\plain \s20\sb100\sa100\keepn\widctlpar \b\f4\fs28\lang1031 Tobias Ratschiller
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 
\par {\b Table of Contents} 
\par 
\par {\b Web Applications
\par Sessions
\par \tab PHP's Built-In Session Library
\par }\pard \fi708\widctlpar {\b A Session's Life
\par Storage Modules
\par Session ID Propagation
\par }\pard \widctlpar {\b Authentication
\par \tab The Login Process
\par }\pard \fi708\widctlpar {\b HTTP-Authentication
\par PHP Authentication}
\par \pard\plain \s19\li-284\sb100\sa100\keepn\widctlpar \b\f4\fs36\lang1031 1. Web Applications 
\par \pard\plain \li-360\widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 To understand the implications of Web application concepts, you need to differentiate between {\i applications} and single{\i  scripts}
. A script is a utility, and as such doesn't have any context. It doesn't know about other scripts in your system. An application, however, is designed to perform more advanced tasks. An application needs to {\i maintain state} and execute {\i 
transactions}. As most applications are made for user, they need to manage different classes of users and therefore need to authenticate them.
\par \pard\plain \s19\li-360\sb100\sa100\keepn\widctlpar \b\f4\fs36\lang1031 2. Sessions 
\par \pard\plain \li-360\widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 Session management is a mechanism to maintain state about a series of requests from the same user across some period of time. 
\par The problem is, that HTTP has no mechanism to maintain state; HTTP is a context-free or stateless protocol. Individual requests aren't related to each other. The Web server (and thus PHP) can't easily distinguish between single users and doesn't know abou
t user sessions. Therefore, we need to find our own way to identify a user and associate session data (that is, all the data you want to store for a user) with the user. We use the term {\i session}
 for a single visit of a user. For example, a typical online shopping session might include logging in, putting an item into the shopping cart, going to the checkout page, entering address and credit card data, submitting the order, and closing the browse
r window. 
\par To associate session data with a user, you need a {\i session identity number} - a key that ties the user to his data. But, as mentioned, HTTP lacks a mechanism to identify users; what should you use, then, to brand the user?
\par {\b PHP's Built-In Session Library} 
\par Luckily, PHP 4.0 has basic session management built in, which frees you from the task of inventing session IDs, serializing and storing session data. While it's very easy and straightforward to use and may suffice for your needs, it lacks some of the adva
nced features that the PHPLib provides. 
\par {\b Figure 1. A basic example of using PHP's built-in sessions} 
\par \pard\plain \s32\li-360\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 session_start(); 
\par print($counter); 
\par $counter++;
\par session_register("counter");
\par \pard\plain \li-360\widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 
This example displays the session ID and a counter that increments each time you access the page. Of course, this example is different from a normal page counter - the session (and thus the counter) is tied to one specific user. With PHP's default configu
ration, the session cookie has a lifetime of {\cs37\f11 0}; if you close the browser and reopen it, the counter restarts from zero, as the cookie has been deleted. 
\par Let's take a closer look at the PHP 4 session functions. PHP's session management library offers the characteristics every session management library needs to have:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab}\pard \fi-360\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}
It stores session data on the server. Because the library uses different storage modules, you can keep the data in plain text files, shared memory, or databases. This reflects exactly what we've explained about storage media - where exactly data is being 
kept is not really important (well, as long the performance of the medium is sufficient).
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab}It uses a cryptographically random session ID to identify a user.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab}It saves the session ID (and only the session ID) on the client side using cookies, {\cs37\f11 GET/POST}, or the script path. (The PHP library provides all of these methods; we show how to use them a little later.)

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab}If the user has disabled cookies, the application can use other means of session propagation.
\par \pard \widctlpar {\b A Session's Life} 
\par A PHP 4 session is started by calling {\cs37\f11 session_start()} or implicitly as soon as you register a session variable with {\cs37\f11 session_register()}
. On start-up, PHP checks whether a valid session ID exists (passed by one of the methods outlined in the later section "Session ID Propagation"). If there's no session ID, a new ID is created. This is also the case when the session is refused and marked 
as invalid because the HTTP referrer for the page comes from a non-local site and {\cs37\f11 extern_referer_check} (note the single "r") is enabled in the PHP configuration. This introduces some additional security, as it prevents users comin
g from other PHP sites taking over a session (which is still highly improbable, however, due to the algorithm used for the generation of the session ID).
\par If a valid ID exists, the frozen variables of that session are reactivated and introduced back to the global namespace. It's as easy to handle session variables as it is to handle {\cs37\f11 GET/POST} variables: if you register a variable named {
\cs37\f11 foo}, {\cs37\f11 $foo} is made accessible automatically after calling {\cs37\f11 session_start()}. Because the {\cs37\f11 serialize()} function was improved in PHP 4, it's also feasible to treat objects (classes) as session variables.
\par All variables you want to preserve across page requests need to be registered to the session library with the {\cs37\f11 session_register()} function. Note that this function takes the {\i name} of a variable as argument, not the variable {\i itself}
. To register the variable {\cs37\f11 $foo}, you'd use this:
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 session_register("foo");
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 This code:
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 session_register($foo);
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 would produce something meaningful only if {\cs37\f11 $foo} was the name of another variable: 
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 $bar = "This is a string";
\par $foo = "bar";
\par session_register($foo);
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 You can use {\cs37\f11 session_unregister()} to remove variables from the session library.
\par As with real life, it's not always easy to tell when a session's life ends - unless it's a violent death, forced by{\cs37\f11  session_destroy()}
. If the session is to die of old age, different configurations need to be taken into consideration. If you propagate the session ID via cookies, the default cookie lifetime is {\cs37\f11 0}, meaning that it will be deleted as soon as the user clo
ses the browser. You can influence the cookie's lifetime with the configuration value {\cs37\f11 lifetime}. Because the server doesn't know whether the cookie still exists on the client side, PHP has another lifetime variable that determines how long{\i 
 after the last access} to this session the data should be destroyed: {\cs37\f11 gc_maxlifetime}. But performing such a cleanup of old sessions (called {\i garbage collection}
) on every page request would cause considerable overhead. Therefore, you can specify with what probability the garbage collection routine should be invoked. If {\cs37\f11 gc_probability} is {\cs37\f11 100}
, the cleanup will be performed on every request (that is, with a probability of 100%); if it's {\cs37\f11 1} as by default, old sessions will be removed with a probability of 1% per request.
\par If you don't use cookies but pass the session ID via {\cs37\f11 GET} or {\cs37\f11 POST}
 instead, you need to pay special attention to the garbage collection routines. People might bookmark URLs containing the session ID, so you need to make sure that sessions are cleaned up often - if the 
session data still exists when the user accesses the page with the session ID at a later time, he'll simply resume the previous session instead of starting with a new session, which may not be your intention. A value of {\cs37\f11 10} to {\cs37\f11 20}
 for {\cs37\f11 gc_probability} would better fit this scenario than the default value of {\cs37\f11 1}.
\par {\b Storage Modules} 
\par To read and save session data, PHP uses storage modules, thus abstracting the back end of the library. There are currently three storage modules available: {\cs37\f11 files}, {\cs37\f11 mm} and {\cs37\f11 user}. By default, PHP uses the {\cs37\f11 files}
 module to save the session data to disk. It creates a text file named after the session ID in {\cs37\f11 /tmp}. In the previous example, the content of this file would look like this, which is a serialized representation of the variable:
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 counter|i:4;
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 You probably won't ever need to access this file directly. 
\par \pard\plain \s20\sb100\sa100\keepn\widctlpar \b\f4\fs28\lang1031 Tip 
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 {\i Serializing} means the transformation of variables to a byte-code representation that can be stored anywhere as a normal string. Without this feature, it wouldn't be possible, for exam
ple, to store PHP arrays into a database. Serializing data is very useful for preserving data across requests - an important facet of a session library. You can use {\cs37\f11 serialize()} and {\cs37\f11 deserialize()}
, but note that in PHP 3 these functions don't work correctly on objects (classes); class functions will be discarded.
\par If you need higher performance, the {\cs37\f11 mm} module is a viable alternative; it stores the data in shared memory and is therefore not limited by the hardware I/O system. The last module, {\cs37\f11 user}, is used inte
rnally to realize user-level callback functions that you define with {\cs37\f11 session_set_save_handler()}. 
\par The real power lies in the capacity to specify user callbacks as storage modules. Because you can write your functions to handle sessions while still being able to rely on the standardized PHP API, you can store sessions wherever and however you want - in
 a database like MySQL, XML files, on a remote FTP server (okay, the latter wouldn't make much sense, but you get the idea).
\par The function {\cs37\f11 session_set_save_handler()} takes six strings as arguments, which must be your callback functions. The syntax of the function is as follows:
\par void session_set_save_handler(string open, string close, string read, string write, string destroy, string gc); 
\par \pard\plain \s20\sb100\sa100\keepn\widctlpar \b\f4\fs28\lang1031 Tip 
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 To leave out one argument, pass an empty string ({\cs37\f11 ""}) to {\cs37\f11 session_set_save_handler()}. 
\par The functions are defined as follows:
\par bool open (string save_path, string sess_name);
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 This function is executed on the
\par initialization of a session; you should use it to prepare your functions, to
\par initialize variables, or the like. It takes two strings as arguments. The first
\par is the path where sessions should be saved. This variable can be specified in
\par php.ini or by the {\cs37 session_save_path()} function - you
\par can use this variable as a joker and use it for module-specific configuration.
\par The second argument is the session's name, by default
\par {\cs37 PHPSESSID}. The {\cs37 open()} function should
\par return {\cs37 true} on success and {\cs37 false} on
\par error.
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 bool close ();
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 This function is executed on shutdown of a session. Use it to free memory or to destroy your variables. It
\par takes no arguments and should return {\cs37 true} on success and
\par {\cs37 false} on error.
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 mixed read (string sess_id, );
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 This important function is called
\par whenever a session is started. It must read out the data of the session
\par identified with {\cs37 sess_id} and return it as a serialized string. If
\par there's no session with this ID, an empty string {\cs37 ""} should be
\par returned. In case of an error, {\cs37 false} should be
\par returned.
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 bool write (string sess_id, , string value);
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 When the session needs to be saved,
\par this function is invoked. The first argument is a string containing the
\par session's ID; the second argument is the serialized representation of the
\par session variables. This function should return {\cs37 true} on success
\par and {\cs37 false} on error.
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 bool destroy (string sess_id, );
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 When the developer calls
\par {\cs37 session_destroy()}, this function is executed. It
\par should destroy all data associated with the session {\cs37 sess_id} and
\par return {\cs37 true} on success and {\cs37 false} on
\par error.
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 bool gc (int max_lifetime, );
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 This function is called on a
\par session's start-up with the probability specified in
\par {\cs37 gc_probability}. It's used for garbage collection; that is, to remove
\par sessions that weren't updated for more than {\cs37 gc_maxlifetime}
\par seconds. This function should return {\cs37 true} on success and
\par {\cs37 false} on error.
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 {\b Session ID Propagation} 
\par PHP 4 sessions support the following methods of passing the session ID:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab}\pard \fi-360\li720\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}Cookies (default)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab}{\cs37\f11 GET/POST} 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab}Hidden in the URL, either done manually or by automatic URL rewriting
\par \pard \widctlpar Cookies are the default way to pass the session ID between pages. If you're happy with cookies, you don't have to worry about any special configuration.
\par Another common way is to pass the ID is with {\cs37\f11 GET/POST}. Your URL would then be similar to {\cs37\f11 script.php3?<session-name>=<session-id>}. You can create such URLs by using the global constant {\cs37\f11 SID}:
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 printf('<a href="script.php?%s">Link</a>', SID);
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 {\i Automatic URL rewriting} is one of the {\i very} cool new features of PHP 4. To enable it, you need to configure PHP with{\cs37\f11  --enable-trans-id} and recompile it. Then the session ID in the form {
\cs37\f11 <session-name>=<session-id>}
 will be added to all relative links within your PHP-parsed pages. While this is a handy feature, it should be used with caution on high-performance sites. PHP has to look at each individual page, analyze it to see whether it contains relative links, and 
eventually add the ID to the links. This obviously introduces a performance penalty. Cookies, on the other hand, are set only once, avoiding the overhead of URL rewriting.
\par \pard\plain \s19\sb100\sa100\keepn\widctlpar \b\f4\fs36\lang1031 3. Authentication 
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 Session management gives you a base to build on. Sophisticated web applications aren't possible without a way to maintain state. 
\par But you'll soon see that you need another technique again and again: authentication. For example, if your users can access some parts of your web site only after having registered themselves with the system, you need authentication.
\par {\b The Login Process} 
\par To be able to authenticate a user, you need his or her identity - you ask the user for an identification. 
Identification is a statement of who the user is: this can be a username, a customer number or anything else, as long as it is unique among your user base.
\par After you got the the information of who the user claims to be, you need to verify the claim. To be able, you need additional data from the user (aside from the claimed identity): an authentication element, commonly a password. To protect against eavesdro
pping, a trusted path - a secure communication channel - is necessary to transmit the password. On the web, this could be SSL.
\par The authentication element is checked against an authentication database. If the authentication returns that the user's identification is correct, the system completes the login process and associates the user's identity and access control information wit
h the user session. In trivial applications, the access control information may merely consist of a flag that the current user is successfully authenticated. In more complex situations, the system may also associate a security leve
l or permission level, defining what the current user is allowed to do in the application (for example, there may a super-user class, or a read-only user class). Depending on the level of needed security, the system needs to log successful or failed login
 attempts; for example, the C2 Security Standard requires systems to audit all login events.
\par {\b HTTP-Authentication} 
\par HTTP provides a method for user authentication: HTTP Basic Authentication. On pages that require authentication, the web server replies with a special header to the client:
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 HTTP/1.1 401 Authorization Required
\par WWW-Authenticate: Basic realm="Protected Area"
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 Upon this, the browser will pop up a modal dialog box asking for username and password. Unfortunately, this type of authentication really merits the "basic" prefix - there are a number of drawbacks:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab}\pard \fi-360\li720\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}To log users out, you have to apply tricks.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab}To log users out after a defined idle time, you have to apply more tricks.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab}If you need groups of user (what we called "permission levels" earlier), you need your own application logic to filter individual users to groups.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab}It's impossible to brand the login process, the gray pop up dialog will always be the same, regardless of the web site.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab}Novice users are generally scared of these dialogs. And you can't provide any help, as you can't modify the dialog.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20 \'b7\tab}HTTP Basic Authentication over PHP is only possible with the module version.
\par \pard \widctlpar All this leads to the conclusion, that it might be wiser to use something else, except of the most basic scenarios.
\par {\b PHP Authentication} 
\par PHP native authentication makes it possible to use arbitrary login screens and authentication procedures. You can write your own authentication library which uses the PHP session management functions we have introduced earlier, or you can use the PHPLib. 
PHPLib can be found at {\field{\*\fldinst REF HYPERLINK "http://phplib.netuse.de/"}{\fldrslt {\b Fehler! Verweisquelle konnte nicht gefunden werden.}}} and it is \rdblquote 
"[...] a toolkit for PHP3 developers supporting them in the development of Web applications"\'94. The main idea of PHPLib is, that there is a 
collection of modules and methods written in PHP for the PHP application programmer's disposal, including session management and authentication. If you want to use PHPLib's authentication library, you also need to use its session management functions, as 
its {\cs37\f11 Session} class is the foundation of all the {\cs37\f11 Auth }class.
\par The PHPLib uses a object oriented approach, which is usually intimidating to new users. If you regularly develop larger applications, however, you're advised to look at the PHPLib; if nothing else, 
it will get you a very profound idea how to solve the most common problems in web application development.
\par To use PHPLib's {\cs37\f11 Auth}
 class, you write a new class which extends the base class and provides your own validation functions and login form. A very simple example, which compares username and password against a hard coded value, could look like the code shown in figure 2.

\par {\b Figure 2. PHPLib's authentcation} 
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 class My_Auth extends Auth 
\par \{
\par     // The class name (serialization helper)
\par     var $classname        = "My_Auth";
\par 
\par     // DB details to use
\par     var $database_class = "DB_Session";
\par     var $database_table = "auth_user";
\par 
\par     // Arbitrary value used in uniqid generation
\par     var $magic = "Foobarbaz";
\par 
\par     function auth_validatelogin() 
\par     \{
\par         // Variables from login form
\par         global $username, $password;
\par         
\par         // Save username
\par         $this->auth["uname"] = $username;
\par 
\par         // Dummy compare
\par         if($username == "kris" && $password == "test")
\par         \{
\par             // Validation successful, store uid
\par             $auth["uid"] = $username;
\par             
\par             // Return user name
\par             return($username);
\par         \}
\par         
\par         return(false);
\par     \}
\par \}
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 In real life, you'll probably want to authenticate against a database with valid logins and provide your own login form in the {\cs37\f11 auth_loginform()}
 function. Now the only thing left is adding an appropriate {\cs37\f11 page_open()} call to the page you want to protect:
\par \pard\plain \s32\widctlpar\tx0\tx959\tx1918\tx2877\tx3836\tx4795\tx5754\tx6713\tx7672\tx8631 \f11\fs20\lang1031 page_open(array("sess" => "My_Session", "auth" => "My_Auth"));
\par \pard\plain \widctlpar \f4\fs20\lang1031 
The first time a user requests a page, he or she will be redirected to a login form and asked to provide username and password. If the authentication is successful, the script continues to be executed below the page_open() call, otherwise PHPLib will stop
 processing with an appropriate error message (which you can also provide yourself).
\par In the next step, you could use the {\cs37\f11 Perm} class to provide permission management facilities. We'd like to refer you to the PHPLib documentation for more details t
hough; now that you've got a basic understanding of how authentication works, it shouldn't be too hard to extend your application yourself.
